{
  "mn37:0.1": "Middle Discourses 37 ",
  "mn37:0.2": "The Shorter Discourse on the Ending of Craving ",
  "mn37:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn37:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in the stilt longhouse of Migāra’s mother in the Eastern Monastery. ",
  "mn37:2.1": "And then Sakka, lord of gods, went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and said to him: ",
  "mn37:2.2": "“Sir, how do you briefly define a mendicant who is freed through the ending of craving, who has reached the ultimate end, the ultimate sanctuary from the yoke, the ultimate spiritual life, the ultimate goal, and is best among gods and humans?” ",
  "mn37:3.1": "“Lord of gods, take a mendicant who has heard: ",
  "mn37:3.2": "‘Nothing is worth insisting on.’ ",
  "mn37:3.3": "When a mendicant has heard that ",
  "mn37:3.4": "nothing is worth insisting on, ",
  "mn37:3.5": "they directly know all things. Directly knowing all things, they completely understand all things. Completely understanding all things, when they experience any kind of feeling—pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral—",
  "mn37:3.6": "they meditate observing impermanence, dispassion, cessation, and letting go in those feelings. ",
  "mn37:3.7": "Meditating in this way, they don’t grasp at anything in the world. ",
  "mn37:3.8": "Not grasping, they’re not anxious. Not being anxious, they personally become extinguished. ",
  "mn37:3.9": "They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be d